l should be saved  to be preserved for future generations  that people not only just read about it  but actually can come and see those  things are they we're in the time when  the Berlin Wall was up you know  the dark side of the wall is the theme  of a nature walk for these school  children they're retracing the route of  the former death strip and they  discovered that the name should be taken  literally from their teacher Maryanne  chabela explains that this wooden board  is a memorial to mark the spot where you  are he Mir was shot dead as he tried to  escape across the border it shows that  he was born on April the 3rd 1945 and  shot on December the 3rd 1964 he was  just 19 years old when he died the  teacher tells his students that you re  mayor had his whole life in front of him  but he was killed because he wanted a  different life these students were born  after the wall came down  but yakit mayor's story makes it easier  for them to grasp the past as they dig  in the ground for a nature project they  stumble upon another piece of history  the students decide to unearth it it  appears to be a fence post from the old  border the 15 year-olds dig up the relic  25 years after the fall of the wall one  of the students points out a hole in the  post where an electric cable used to  pass through the cable registered every  movement information that was sent to  the watchtower that's still standing a  few meters away as well as teaching  Miriam Sybilla looks after the tower  found objects from the surrounding  forest are on display bits of the border  fence and spiked metz known as Stalin  grass which are today a rarity  Marianne says the German Historical  Museum in Bonn wanted the Stalin grass  as an exhibit but they refused to give  it away 25 years after the wall crumbled  in a political sense its physical  presence can still be found  you
 

